React + Database / Final Project
37. React + Prisma / Backend

At this stage, React can be connected to a real database through a backend.

Previously, the basic API flow was:

React
  ↓
fetch()
  ↓
API
  ↓
Data

Now the application can use a backend and database:

React
  ↓
fetch()
  ↓
Backend / API
  ↓
Prisma
  ↓
MySQL Database

React normally should not connect directly to MySQL. Instead, React sends requests to the backend, and the backend communicates with the database.

For example, React can request:

GET /api/products

The backend receives the request and uses Prisma to retrieve the products.

A Prisma query might look like:

const products = await prisma.product.findMany();

The backend can then send the products back to React.

React can receive them with:

async function getProducts() {
  const response = await fetch("/api/products");
  const data = await response.json();

  return data;
}

This is also a good opportunity to review async and await.

API and database operations take time, so JavaScript needs to handle them asynchronously.

For example:

async function getProducts() {
  const response = await fetch("/api/products");
  const data = await response.json();

  console.log(data);
}

The overall idea is:

React
→ requests data

Backend
→ receives request

Prisma
→ communicates with database

MySQL
→ stores the data
38. Database CRUD

CRUD represents the four main operations performed on data:

C → Create
R → Read
U → Update
D → Delete

These operations were already introduced with APIs. Now they can be performed on a real MySQL database through Prisma.

Create

Create a new record:

await prisma.product.create({
  data: {
    name: "Keyboard",
    price: 50
  }
});

This creates a new product in the database.

Read

Get data:

const products = await prisma.product.findMany();

The backend can return the products to React.

Update

Change an existing record:

await prisma.product.update({
  where: {
    id: 1
  },
  data: {
    price: 60
  }
});

This changes the price of product 1.

Delete

Remove a record:

await prisma.product.delete({
  where: {
    id: 1
  }
});

This removes product 1 from the database.

The complete CRUD relationship is:

CREATE → Add new data
READ   → Get data
UPDATE → Change existing data
DELETE → Remove data

The complete application flow can now look like:

React Form
    ↓
POST request
    ↓
Backend
    ↓
Prisma
    ↓
MySQL

And when retrieving data:

MySQL
    ↓
Prisma
    ↓
Backend
    ↓
JSON
    ↓
fetch()
    ↓
React
    ↓
Screen

One important difference between using a normal JavaScript array and a database is that database data is persistent.

For example:

const products = [];

Data stored only in this array can disappear when the application stops.

A database stores the data separately:

Application closes
        ↓
Application opens again
        ↓
Database still contains the data
