40. Final Project Polish / Presentation

The final stage should focus on improving the project rather than adding many new features.

UI Polish

Check the overall interface.

For example:

Are buttons easy to understand?

Are forms organized?

Are titles clear?

Is spacing consistent?

Can important actions be easily found?

Instead of:

[Button]

use something clearer:

[Add Game]

The interface should make it obvious what each action does.

Loading and Error Handling

The application should handle situations where data takes time to load or something goes wrong.

For example:

Loading...

or:

Failed to load games.

If there is no data:

No games found.

After an action:

Game successfully added.

or:

Game successfully deleted.

This makes the application feel more complete.

Form Validation

Forms should check whether the information entered is valid.

For example:

Game Name:
[                  ]

Genre:
[                  ]

[Add Game]

If the name is empty:

Game name is required.

Validation should clearly explain what needs to be corrected.

Responsive Design

The application should also be checked at different screen sizes:

Desktop
   ↓
Tablet
   ↓
Mobile

It does not need to look like a professional commercial website. The main goal is that the interface remains usable on different screen sizes.

Code Organization

Before finishing, check whether the project is easy to understand.

A possible structure is:

src/
├── components/
│   ├── Navbar.jsx
│   ├── GameCard.jsx
│   └── Button.jsx
│
├── pages/
│   ├── Home.jsx
│   ├── Games.jsx
│   └── GameDetail.jsx
│
├── hooks/
│   └── useFetch.jsx
│
├── context/
│   └── FavoritesContext.jsx
│
├── services/
│   └── gameApi.js
│
├── App.jsx
└── main.jsx

Look for:

Very large components
Repeated code
Unused imports
Confusing variable names
API code repeated in many places
Components that could be reused

The goal is to make the project easier to read and maintain.

Final Presentation

The final presentation can be around 5–10 minutes.

Start by explaining what the project is.

For example:

"My project is a Game Library."

Then explain what the application can do:

Users can view games,
search for games,
add games,
edit games,
and delete games.

Next, explain the basic technical structure:

React
 ↓
Backend API
 ↓
Prisma
 ↓
MySQL

Then demonstrate the application:

Open application
      ↓
View data
      ↓
Add item
      ↓
Edit item
      ↓
Delete item

Finally, explain a few important technical features.

For example:

"I used React Router for multiple pages."

"I used fetch to communicate with the backend."

"I used Prisma to communicate with MySQL."

"I used React state to control the UI."

There is no need to explain every line of code. The important part is being able to explain how the different parts of the project work together.

